# /home/standby/.ssh/authorized_keys on the STANDBY.
#
# The key can do exactly one thing: rsync into /opt, write-only. It cannot get
# a shell, cannot read anything back out, cannot forward ports.
#
#   -wo   write-only: the primary may push, but this key cannot pull data out
#   /opt  the only path it may touch
#
# rrsync ships with rsync (often at /usr/share/rsync/scripts/rrsync or
# /usr/bin/rrsync). Without it, a "restricted" key is just a shell.

command="/usr/bin/rrsync -wo /opt",restrict ssh-ed25519 AAAA... warm-sync@primary

# A second key for the one diagnostic the primary needs — checking that the
# standby is alive — kept separate so the sync key stays single-purpose.
command="/usr/bin/df -h /opt",restrict ssh-ed25519 AAAA... warm-check@primary
